<p class="lead">
  Printing a PDF form, filling it out by hand, scanning it back in, and emailing the scan is still how a surprising number of people complete digital paperwork — largely because they assume that's the only option. If the PDF has real fillable fields (most government, HR, and application forms do), you can fill it out directly and skip printing entirely.
</p>

<h2>Fill it out in your browser (no upload, free)</h2>
<p>
  The <a href="/tools/fill-pdf-form/">Fill PDF Form tool</a> on keptlocal reads a PDF's actual form fields and lets you fill them in, entirely on your device.
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Drop your PDF form onto the tool.</li>
  <li>The tool reads every fillable field — text boxes, checkboxes, dropdowns — and shows them as a clean list.</li>
  <li>Fill in each field.</li>
  <li>Leave <strong>Flatten after filling</strong> checked (the default) to lock in your answers, or uncheck it to keep the fields editable.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Fill &amp; download PDF</strong>.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How to tell if a PDF actually has fillable fields</h2>
<p>
  Not every PDF that looks like a form has real form fields behind it. A quick way to check before uploading anywhere: open the PDF in a standard viewer and click where you'd expect to type — if a text cursor appears and you can type directly into a bordered box, it has real AcroForm fields. If nothing happens when you click, the "form" is just an image or flat layout with blank lines drawn on it, and there's no field data for any tool (this one included) to read. In that case, the practical options are printing and filling by hand, or using a PDF editor that lets you place free-floating text boxes manually.
</p>

<h2>Why flattening matters</h2>
<p>
  An unflattened, filled-in PDF form is still, technically, a blank form with values sitting in its fields — anyone who opens it in a PDF editor can change those values without any indication that they've been altered. For casual internal use that's rarely a problem. For anything you're submitting as a record of what you attested to — a signed application, a declaration, a form with legal weight — flattening converts your answers into fixed page content, the same as any other text on the page, so what you submitted is what stays submitted.
</p>
<p>
  The tradeoff: once flattened, the fields are gone — you (or anyone else) can't go back and edit just one answer without starting over from an unflattened copy. Keep the original blank form if you might need to fill it out differently later.
</p>

<h2>Privacy: what happens to your form data</h2>
<p>
  Everything — reading the fields, rendering the inputs, writing your answers back into the PDF — happens locally using pdf-lib in your browser tab. Nothing is uploaded. This matters more than usual for forms specifically, since they routinely ask for information you'd rather not send through an unnecessary third-party server: a Social Security number, a medical history question, a salary figure.
</p>

